  "body": "We added the width/height attributes in https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/5894, and so now we should map these two attributes to width/height/aspect-ratio CSS property value of `<img>` elements. However, the spec is unclear if the user only provide one attribute (i.e. only width or only height). So how do we handle the missing property values? For example:\r\n```\r\n<picture>\r\n  <source srcset= \"green200x200.png\" width=\"100\"></source>\r\n  <img width=\"50\" height=\"10\">\r\n</picture>\r\n```\r\nWe don't specify height attribute of the source element, so should we map the height attribute of `<img>` element to its height CSS property value, or override it with the initial value of height (i.e. auto)? In other words, `getComputedStyle(img).height` should be 10px, or auto?\r\n\r\nThe wpt ([picture-aspect-ratio.html](https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt/blob/321bbd64536947a851b62a1af992efd8f8efc1fe/html/rendering/replaced-elements/attributes-for-embedded-content-and-images/picture-aspect-ratio.html#L114-L123)) expects the result is auto. I think this makes sense and I believe both Blink and WebKit follow this rule, and so Gecko will also follow this.\r\n\r\nHowever, it'd be better to address this in the spec I think. Do we have any other concerns? Or this is already in the spec (i.e. [embedded-content.html#the-source-element](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/embedded-content.html#the-source-element)) but I missed? Thanks.",
